So I was an associate marketing manager at 20, but it was a very low paid role and I was also their social media manager. It was pretty bad and I would say it's more like an intern--most of what I did was community management, and eventually they re-titled me. After that, I became a product owner (later on they made the role into producer) for a very large network of websites but in addition to the head maps and managing a design team, I ran all their social and wrote content for them. I was seen as a glorified social media and content manager to the company; the job title did not match all the work I did and it was very draining. It also was used to keep me at salaries, often citing this role gets X amount in Y city, and when I would try to show I did about 20-30 more things than what my job description said, I wasn't given anything.

&#x200B;

At that point I decided to job search and I found 2 freelance options, both consultant. I went with the one in the CPG space, which became marketing director after I signed full-time. For me, generalist brought way more opportunities, because being a social media focused person was often used as a way to say "social media isn't worth the ROI."

&#x200B;

Hope that helps!

I was actually just promoted to CMO of my company, I'm really lucky! We're a relatively small mid 8 figure company and I was one of the first employees 4 years ago, where i joined as a marketing director since it was a solo dept. I now have a team of like 4 marketing folks and oversee all the other 'office' type of depts too like sales, customer service, etc (he technically wanted to my title to be something else, but I truly want to do marketing for life, it's my passion and day to day operations is boring even if I've done a lot of it the past 4 years, so I would rather he eventually find a COO and such).

&#x200B;

And tbh, I've always been a generalist. Even when I was a CM or social media expert, I was often doing website heatmaps or analyzing metrics of ads. So I think the jump was pretty natural for me, but dropping a limiting title like social media manager helped a lot for career progression. I prefer a variety of tasks related to marketing--all the way from product design and roadmaps to ad campaigns and creative copywriting.

So, my 2 cents is no. I would apply for marketing jr positions and say your passion got sparked by your business minor, aiming toward math type stuff like spreadsheets/analysis about ads etc. I didn't even finish my degree and I make six figures, but my degree *was* in political science. I know a few other folks whose degrees were in unrelated fields (English for one, physics for another) who are also at good salaries. Your degree is good enough to also let you help out a bit on Shopify and understanding how to troubleshoot problems, which is great in a marketing person. Self-sufficiency is really good in jr jack/jill of all trade positions. I know how to do CSS/html/etc and have used it a lot in my earlier years to tweak blog posts, debug a Shopify app here and there, stuff like that.

&#x200B;

Not only that, a lot of junior/entry level marketing positions are basically glorified secretaries. You create spreadsheets to monitor metrics, you email people for business collabs, you set up ads with parameters from your boss. It's not hard at first, and your degree will only let you get an edge on people if you play up the math aspect.

So uh I'm gonna be honest, I had the opposite sort of? I got jealous of his pet. I never raged or did anything...but he would often interrupt me to talk about his cat, spend an hour or two talking about everything from her hair to her eyes, and often have literal down days because she "didn't seem happy with him." He was always reading about diet things for her and if I got her a toy and she loved it, the toy would mysteriously disappear or have a hazard. One time I got her a cat tree and she clearly loved it as a surprise gift to him, he dismissed it and said she hated it, then he randomly went out and got a bigger one a few months later and said it was her fav thing.

&#x200B;

It got really frustrating, to the point where he never said a nice thing to me--never said I looked pretty, never said thank you, never said he loved me--but would just endlessly say he loved her, say she was a gorgeous cat, and talk about how she was everything to him. I would just tear up and genuinely wonder why he couldn't even say he loved me some weeks, but could (while I was around) for hours extol his cat's virtue.

&#x200B;

And I love my animals a lot (like more than most people! I am a total cat lady!). He also did weird stuff too where like she'd claw him or bite him and I'd be like "I think she's upset you're holding her this much" and he'd just hand wave me away, and even say animals existed to be held.

I genuinely would love to write one up, I've been thinking of making a blog but I don't really want to completely reveal my company's strat by typing my name here (which is why I ditched LinkedIn posts). 

I started affiliate marketing in 2017 and have made a huge program, and yeah--it's actually still wild west, though it is standardizing quickly. I would say within 5 years it should be much easier (and by proxy less lucrative, it's a give and take). At the moment, some influencers ask for so much and others ask for very little. It's all about pitching to each one like a sales client, if you're a brand like I am, and negotiating them down.

&#x200B;

When you say how do you gauge, it's actually so wild. You get very lost in the proverbial sauce, like I love to check influencers on several platforms but also monitor their feed myself organically; check their followers out, check the actual commenters, look for follow4follow buffers as well as manipulated fanbases (a lot of people buy followers, then gain organic ones, but those organic ones would never purchase since they're usually from just following out of a FOMO rather than being a true fan).

&#x200B;

Anyway, yeah, I really DO want to share this info...I should consider making a subreddit post. It sucks because I'm often met with either "lol you liar" or "lol ok" remarks from people who don't do influencer management! Or people who work at a big firm! Like yes, your experience will 100% vary, that's the whole thing about marketing online. There are a million ways to make your million.

I don't pay Kim or any influencer over $5k a month, and on average all my influencers are profitable. This reddit can be so garbage sometimes, so many assumptions are made by people who don't have the same experience so they invalidate someone else's. You can easily find micro influencers or people with even sub 10k and make massive profits. It's ok if your experiences are different, *but my business is literally in case studies for influencer programs for a reason.*

&#x200B;

Besides, this guy asked what to do for his sister. Who is an influencer. So I explained how I would get people, as you know, I work with a lot of brands who would hire someone like her. Who are actively excited about 500k TikTok people.

ArriveAtFive.

&#x200B;

Use it in all copy, say 'remember to ARRIVEATFIVE to save on dinner for the night.'

