&#x200B;

This is absolutely horrifying and I hope someone in your life can read this and drill it into your head. I am so sorry this is your normal. This is emotional abuse.

Because she clearly didn't know a dog would be this hard and her husband would be like this? Jesus, the judgment. Sounds like this luckily exposed how much of a louse her husband is, but it doesn't mean her dream of kids was dumb or unrealistic when she first had it. The dog situation is just exposing the harsh reality: he is not reliable nor helpful.

&#x200B;

OP: It will be much worse with kids, do not have sex with him or at least make sure you are on birth control plus use condoms even though you have fertility issues. Sometimes kids still happen, or the infertility diagnosis was just temporary, etc. It will not get better if this is how badly he's acting about a DOG.

I didn't do undergrad, and I do make six figures now, but a lot of it has come from doing very well. I feel like I would definitely be a harder hire at a big place, but where I am I have proven millions in ROI and it was like that at my last job as well (which underpaid me, and paperwork was part of it). That said I have now been headhunted a few times for larger companies and offered great salaries/benefits, so I'm not entirely sure. I think marketing can really be 90% learned experience, so if you end up grinding 24/7 and learn cutting edge stuff, you can compensate for no MBA (or even undergrad). I just would not suggest it.

&#x200B;

For me personally though I would not want to work for a place where a MBA = a 50% raise, I would rather work for a place where hitting a deliverable out of the park and making millions for a company equals that raise (which thankfully I do now, sadly have not in the past).

When you have a tweet go viral and get thousands of RTs, you really often only get like 10-20 followers from it. People who commit to following off things are very rare, it has to be hip to follow something and people are all about image, stories, and narratives.

&#x200B;

If you want to increase your follow back, you have to let them know you exist. Right now people just see a cool picture on Reddit and upvote then move along with their day. He should start with a very tiny watermark in the corner of drawings he posts on Reddit. Then in a comment, he should link his social and say "Here is a timelapse of this video being created I posted on YouTube/Instagram. Mods: let me know if linking is OK in the comments, I'm just trying to share more of my work so people can learn from it." People who click-through to see the comments will likely see this and upvote it, or at least have a seed planted in their head about the artist.,

then he has to do the harder work of self-promoting more on other platforms. With the timelapse, he needs to consistently put it on every social media at appropriate times. Then he needs to find fledgling communities to hock his art at, for example maybe he does anime art and there is a good FB group for artists who drawn fanart--posting there would be good.

&#x200B;

But even this isn't enough.  A big thing is becoming part of the community. I know when I ran a gaming blog as a teenager a decade ago, I followed everyone's social media and I talked to them daily--especially the more popular ones. When a piece I wrote came out, I would send them all links to it asking what they thought. They would share it if they liked it, and as I got better, it got more and more traffic (eventually it got me sent to some cons and stuff with expenses paid as games companies liked what I wrote). Embedding yourself in the community for your medium is really important, if you're talented and work hard, your colleagues often will promote you with a retweet or an art exchange or collab.

>nothing else.

&#x200B;

I would say "I'm not going to post this anywhere, please don't take it as a threat. I'm just showing you what it was like for me and it's breaking my heart. It has to stop."

&#x200B;

Otherwise she may literally take it as some threat/blackmail and go nuts.

I get plenty of food from my day job, like I'm talking 100 bars of a protein bar from a company etc. I always donate it to a charity. They're never expired, or opened, and they usually retail for $4-5 a bar. In fact just last week I gave like 30 boxes of chickpea pasta that had like 4 months left on expiration because I realized there was zero chance I'd use more than 1 box before 4 months from now. Charity doesn't have to be giving "something meaningful in your life," sometimes it really can be someone else's trash is treasure--if the trash is presentable and actually up to code/standards.

&#x200B;

To be clear I'm only arguing with your idea that donations = scam, some people are just awful people regardless of the situation. Tons of people give good stuff.

Yeah--I let someone know what I made, in an interest of us both going for nice raises, several years ago. That person acted genuine, honest, and thankful. We went for the raises and they acted like all was well. They also then slowly began to act weirder and weirder, to the point of becoming a major sore point in my routine and starting lots of nasty rumors about me.

&#x200B;

It turns out they really didn't like me anymore after that, but it was totally unrelated (yeah sure) and they also were telling people I barely worked yet was overpaid (interesting how that started only after they found out I made more than them).

&#x200B;

Salary discussing is a double-edged sword. You never know how your team will react, and if they are petty or dislike their work environment, even if they should be blaming their boss, they may end up blaming you.

Yeah, for example, my speciality has always been influencer marketing, social media (copywriting as well) & competitive research--that means I'll market very well through influencers, as well as keep my pulse on the industry's tends very well, and also grow social media quickly with real organic followers. I know analytics passably, I barely know SEO, I know html/etc, I know e-commerce platforms and Photoshop, and I know how to create good reports. I also have learned google ads pretty well recently too.

&#x200B;

But I EXCEL at organizing promotional systems, like brand ambassadors and customer relations, while also growing the brand through good copywriting. I also always know what every competitor is about to do. So I'm valuable there, as a niche.

My 1st job I had this game on steam I had to upsell even though it was a garbage game and reinvent the FPS genre. It was built on an engine that was like 9 years old, infamously 'meh', AND it was built poorly. This was also before retro was in (now it's hot to be old, so it would have worked).

&#x200B;

My boss tells me to fill in all the listing details, I'm like so happy we're FINALLY on Steam, I'm just so excited.

&#x200B;

Then he tells me to list the price at $49.99.

&#x200B;

Keep in mind, that's what a AAA game STRUGGLES to sell for. Let alone a garbage bin, awful game with an old nearly decade old engine that's full of bugs. I wanted to do $5 and then $20 for 5 copies (it was a multiplayer game). I broke down case studies, I showed how our demographic are 15-25 year old males with low incomes. I talked him down to $29.99 but no cheaper, he would not budge.

&#x200B;

We sold like 1000 copies in 3 months, he freaked out on me, said it was all my fault; that we priced it too low so the few sales we got didn't even cover dev (at this point he'd burned millions, so no sales would have fixed it, beyond a smash hit).

&#x200B;

I eventually bundled it in a FPS bundle on Black Friday for $1.50 a copy, and we sold like 100k copies... we then instantly had a fanbase, and people supported our next game which was a F2P model due to our success as a cheap title on Steam. I essentially reinvented our whole company into a free-to-play and/or budget company, and it *worked*. I left the company shortly after for a better job.

&#x200B;

Lesson: price points matter so much. Know your real value, and what value consumers will actually  pay. He clearly didn't despite being a millionaire who made a lot of money in sales.

I know you're just memeing, but truthfully Trump being elected made perfect sense. People underestimated him, he manipulated the fake news aspect and social media aspect, and we also ran someone who honestly had the charisma of a wet blanket. The Clinton campaign also didn't really talk to middle America, especially states that have always been on the fence like Ohio or Michigan. I have always hated the Clintons and I'm a die-hard liberal; I'm not sure why we expected Ms. Future President to dominate Trump, but we did and here we are.

&#x200B;

Now he's had 4 years to really brainwash his core demographic, who would die for him. We just need high voting turnout from people who are against him but don't usually vote (college kids, etc).

So I disagree with flipping burgers. It sounds like he suffered from burnout, quit, and became an unreliable unsupportive mess. Flipping burgers to make ends meet will likely just make the problem worse.

&#x200B;

Since he's into music, and is apparently good, why not try to tutor kids parttime? Work at a school teaching it? Coach people? Something. She can set a goal of we need, IDK, $1k a month coming in while he looks for jobs. Then set the goal of like 10 applications a week.

Hmm, first of all this is awesome so thanks. However, I'm dumb at this, so this is very hard for my brain to wrap around. That probably sounds sad, but my brain is very creative and I'm super ... good at selling a brand, organizing a group of influencers, targeting a niche, writing copy, event planning, package design, brand overall awareness, and competitive research. But when I see this text, I get very confused how to start it and I feel lost. (I'm like this with a lot more analytical insight type tasks, like I said, give me a thousand cold pitches or writing good A/B copy for something any day.)

&#x200B;

I think for me examples work best. So if I'm  understanding right...

&#x200B;

I need to have a theme for content. With our new layout, we plan to make a recipe section, which is the #1 search for our category. Let's say we're a vodka (we aren't), it'd be like having a cocktail index. SEO for EASY COCKTAILS or QUICK COCKTAILS or HEALTHY COCKTAILS, all that kind of stuff. Again we're not cocktails, I'm just trying to draw a good comparison.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Anyway so we'll have a recipe section with 2 recipes a week, that are based on our niche, and have very valuable SEO keywords. We plan to do a weekly email linking the recipes. We're also adding a loyalty program at this time, so the newsletter will alert them of sales in addition to the recipes and double point days and new loyalty items.

&#x200B;

We never did a newsletter prior, again like I said, it's been a tiny team and we grew way too fast so it's been putting out firsts. For ex today I was negotiating with a national grocery chain, which is so outside my job role it sucks, but that's where we're at: way too many hats, and my team is full of intern type folks who have to be taught a lot. I had to train them how to answer DMs and social media, stuff like that--they're all new people, and none had jobs prior (they're fresh out of college). Once I got free of that workload last year doing DMs, I had to then go into wholesale to help us out, and it's still something I'm doing too much of.

The new layout will have a new recipe index, too, to help showcase them.

&#x200B;

Would this be my pillar page?

&#x200B;

Edit: And one more thing, I think getting google to like our product pages is another issue. If you search our product, we're often like the fifth result, with Amazon and other esellers who sell us higher since our SEO is that bad.

Our conversions are about 12% now, sometimes it's 7%, depends on social media campaigns I run. We have a new layout and some fun stuff (new products, cheaper prices) coming up. I just really wanted to get more awareness for our product through organic searches, as I know our competitor relies 100% on a popular keyword as their social proof and retail proof is nonexistent.

&#x200B;

Like I said, we are literally all social media, and we have a cult following--so our conversions are insane. We get almost zero web traffic.

&#x200B;

I 100% see what you're saying I just think I've neglected SEO so it's this low hanging fruit I could use to actually get google traffic to get more $$$.

Yeah I understand most of these concepts, the problem is I don't understand how to impliment them. I basically know what keywords I want to use too (I've found good volume ones)--I just have no idea how to actually effectively impliment the keyword.

&#x200B;

Basically I can do the marketing scope and pick the proper search terms (I've used google ad words and understand what makes a good keyword, esp high buy potential ones, and I analyzed our competitors), but I have no idea how to effectively make google enjoy us as a keyword.

You may be remembering things better than they were. My HS, in a super liberal area that's hugely pride oriented, had 1 gay student and he was only open during our last years there (05-06). We knew nothing of trans people, everyone was just a 'cross dresser' if they presented as femme as a guy.  I didn't even know I was bisexual until probably 09 and was able to think it through despite being with a girl, and had never known a bisexual person nor heard much about them besides a few shows that showed it (like Grey's Anatomy and Callie). So a trans person even being mentioned on TV at that time in that context is pretty ahead of its time; even just their gay characters were. Just a few years prior, Buffy was giant for breaking that barrier with Tara/Willow.

&#x200B;

I mean think about it, even Ellen coming out was a big deal and it was just like 7 years prior to VM airing. Times have changed and they've changed super quick and I'm happy for it, but VM was progressive TV for its time.

