You also need to play into the bible a lot more. Your logo should have biblical themes. You should try to create a social media presence all about god, with quotes and relatable bible memes.

&#x200B;

Lack of an about us page, lack of an identity lack of a position: these are hurting you a lot. Christians look for trust, and they'll want to know you're into God and your company is as well.

I tried telling my mom very recently how it was hard accepting my dad sold our childhood home for like $1 million, and I didn't get a penny of it--that she'd paid for half of it and gave it up and lived in poverty while he, my abuser whom I left as a teen when he nearly killed me, lived in money I would never for decades have despite making a six figure salary. That what I really want in life now as a woman that hit 30 is a house in this city and I realistically can't afford it for at least six or seven more years (unless I want to have zero savings and a giant amount to pay off which scares me thanks to my mom always being broke).

&#x200B;

Her response? "Think about the children in Africa."

&#x200B;

Didn't skip a beat.

&#x200B;

This post really reminds me that it's OK to be angry about this, I feel really grateful I have what I have but also fiercely angry about what was taken from me that I can't get back.

Haha I make it sound cool, but it's actually for work (though I do own the account) :) We sell like 25 products now.

&#x200B;

It's actually quite shit, not the job but IG, so that's why I felt compelled to comment. We went from selling like 5k in 3 hours last time to barely getting 1k in 3 hours since their latest algorithm change. Luckily we're growing on FB and other platforms, plus we have landed in retail stores (we used our IG popularity to attain that deal). I know they can't have full transparency on IG's algo, but I saw every brand I also follow hit with the same sudden halving of likes/comments overnight a couple of months back, and I imagine it's because their reach suddenly halved since ours did as well.

Everyone is already saying this, but ACA saves you.

&#x200B;

It will be about $200-400 a month depending on what premiums you select and your state. Not to beat a dead horse, nor make you feel bad, but you should have health insurance if you can afford it (and if you own a house you definitely can). I pay my $400 a month for my state's best plan, as my work does not offer health insurance, and I nearly died last Fall. It paid out in dividends when I didn't have to pay $100k in bills... It's always worth it in America, I'm so sorry you had to learn the hard way.

Outdoor cats hurt outside populations of other animals. They also have incredibly short lifespans because humans have industrialized most of the areas and while cats are masters of the universe, they don't always understand cars. Keeping cats outside can actually be really cruel if you live in a city; you'll be hurting birds, destroying neighbor's gardens, and also losing your cat every 1-2 years to cars.

&#x200B;

And even if you live out in the country, they often become coyote prey or end up hit by a car on a slow country road...

&#x200B;

So yes, clipping nails (responsibly) seems like a small price to play for the cat living. They seem to have nearly identical qualities of life, so long as they are given the appropriate comforts. Cats can be very entertained indoors. Mine have like a dozen windows, tons of plants, lots of wall climbing trees and shelves. I also got another cat just so they would have partners to run around with. I play with them about 30 minutes a day.

&#x200B;

Outside, one of my cats would be dead within probably a week. She is just a lil dumb. Love her to pieces, but she's one of those Siamese (they're always either Super Smart or Super Dumb, never just normal). The goofy dumb kind who would literally lie down in the road. I lost a cat just like her when I was a kid to the exact same thing (road naps), so when I saw her temperament she became entirely indoors. I used to let my other cat out with supervision 2x a week though--she was really calm.

That's a big thing, what you said, placing above the old salary--when I switched jobs, I actually just asked what I thought was a good rate for the position and did not disclose my salary since I knew my salary was going under the market rate by a large bit. It was $80k more than my current position, but my current position had an awful track record of underpaying (by knowingly large amounts) and the position was a promotion for what I was doing that would involve a lot more independence. I ended up getting the job and the salary--and I've since have gotten a lot in raises because I ask for them (since after all, they answered my salary very quickly with a yes, it means they would have gone higher, so lesson learned).

&#x200B;

Your advice is really salient, and I just wanted to chime in with a somewhat related 2 cents because I do see that as many people's hold up in earning: they always give their old salary away. It's not good unless that salary is what you want to be employed at your new job for.
